.SH NAME
ns_urlspace \- Assign Application Data to URLs
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBns_urlspace set\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? ?\fB-id \fIinteger\fR\fR? ?\fB-key \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? \fIURL\fR \fIdata\fR
.sp
\fBns_urlspace get\fR ?\fB-context \fIsetId\fR\fR? ?\fB-exact\fR? ?\fB-id \fIinteger\fR\fR? ?\fB-key \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? \fIURL\fR
.sp
\fBns_urlspace list\fR ?\fB-id \fIinteger\fR\fR?
.sp
\fBns_urlspace unset\fR ?\fB-allconstraints\fR? ?\fB-id \fIinteger\fR\fR? ?\fB-key \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?\fB-recurse\fR? \fIURL\fR
.sp
\fBns_urlspace new\fR
.sp
.BE
.SH DESCRIPTION
The command \fBns_urlspace\fR allows you to assign, query, list, or
delete data in the trie data structure representing the URL space\&. It
supports filters and inheritance, enabling you to assign data to
specific URL patterns (e\&.g\&., all "\&.adp" pages) or to assign data to a
higher-level node, which can be inherited by all nodes below it\&.
.PP
The values assigned to the URL space are shared among all threads of
a server\&. This using this command is somewhat similar to the use of
\fBnsv\fR but differs in its inheritance and filtering capabilities\&.
.PP
This command allows you, for example, to implement access control
(similar to the \fInsperm module\fR [\&.\&./\&.\&./nsperm/files/nsperm\&.html])
at the Tcl level\&. Other applications include blacklisting certain
parts of the URL tree from statistics, or assigning different master
templates to different parts of the URL tree\&.
.PP
The \fBns_urlspace\fR command provides scripting access to
functionality that has been part of NaviServer for a long time\&. For
example, the mapping of requests to connection thread pools is
performed using the same mechanism\&. In this case, the mapping of URLs
to pools can be performed via the command \fBns_server\fR, or it can
be provided via configuration variables in the pool section (see,
e\&.g\&., \fITuning NaviServer\fR [\&.\&./\&.\&./manual/files/admin-tuning\&.html])\&.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBns_urlspace set\fR ?\fB-constraints \fIconstraints\fR\fR? ?\fB-id \fIinteger\fR\fR? ?\fB-key \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? \fIURL\fR \fIdata\fR
Assigns a \fIvalue\fR to a node in the URL space specified by
\fIURL\fR\&. By default, the value is inherited by all sub-nodes, unless the
option \fB-noinherit\fR is specified\&.
.sp
The \fIURL\fR can include glob-style patterns (e\&.g\&., "/*\&.adp") to match multiple URLs\&.
.sp
The option \fB-constraints\fR allows you to
specify additional context constraints for the assignment (see
\fBContext Constraints\fR)\&.
.CS


 # Assign values to URL patterns
 ns_urlspace set /*\&.adp             A
 ns_urlspace set /*\&.html            H
 ns_urlspace set /internal/space/*  I

 # Assign a value with a context constraints
 ns_urlspace set -constraints {"user-agent" "*bot*"} /*\&.adp C

.CE
.TP
\fBns_urlspace get\fR ?\fB-context \fIsetId\fR\fR? ?\fB-exact\fR? ?\fB-id \fIinteger\fR\fR? ?\fB-key \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? \fIURL\fR
Retrieves a value from the URL space for a specified \fIURL\fR\&. By
default, the returned value might be inherited from a parent node in
the trie structure\&.
.sp
When the option \fB-exact\fR is used, inheritance is
deactivated, and only values directly assigned to the \fIURL\fR are
returned\&. When the option \fB-noinherit\fR is specified, only
values set with the \fB-noinherit\fR flag are considered\&.
.sp
The option \fB-context\fR allows you to specify context
constraints for the retrieval (see \fBContext Constraints\fR)\&.
If this option is not specified, the server tries to
use the current connection context, if available\&. This requires that
a connection is open and the socket is connected\&. Otherwise, no
context is provided\&.
.CS


 # Retrieve mappings without context
 ns_urlspace get /test\&.adp
 # Returns: A

 ns_urlspace get /internal/space/documents/info\&.html
 # Returns: I

 # Retrieve mapping with context
 set context [ns_set create "user-agent" "googlebot"]
 ns_urlspace get -context $context /test\&.adp
 # Returns: C

.CE
.TP
\fBns_urlspace list\fR ?\fB-id \fIinteger\fR\fR?
Lists the values assigned to the URL space, which is either the
default URL space or the one specified by \fB-id\fR\&. The command returns a
list of lists, where each item contains the following elements:
.RS
.IP \(bu
\fBkey\fR - The key associated with the assignment\&.
.IP \(bu
\fBpath\fR - The URL pattern (e\&.g\&., "/internal/space")\&.
.IP \(bu
\fBfilter pattern\fR - Any constraints applied (e\&.g\&., "*\&.adp")\&.
.IP \(bu
\fBinherit\fR or \fBnoinherit\fR - Whether inheritance is enabled\&.
.IP \(bu
?contextfilter? - The optional contextfilter
.IP \(bu
\fBdata\fR - The assigned value\&.
.RE
.CS


 ns_urlspace list
 Returns: {\&. / *\&.html inherit H} {\&. / *\&.adp inherit A} {\&. / *\&.adp inherit {user-agent *bot*} C} {\&. /internal/space * inherit I}

.CE
.TP
\fBns_urlspace unset\fR ?\fB-allconstraints\fR? ?\fB-id \fIinteger\fR\fR? ?\fB-key \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-noinherit\fR? ?\fB-recurse\fR? \fIURL\fR
Unset a value for a \fIURL\fR for the given or default key\&. This
command undoes the effects of \fBns_urlspace set\fR\&.
.sp
The option \fB-allconstraints\fR implies that all context
filters are deleted\&. When the option \fB-noinherit\fR is used,
only the values set with \fB-noinherit\fR are deleted, and vice
versa\&.
.sp
The option \fB-recurse\fR performs the operation
recursively on all sub-nodes under the specified \fIURL\fR\&. Unless
\fB-recurse\fR is specified, the command returns 1 on success or
0 on failure\&.
.TP
\fBns_urlspace new\fR
Allocates a new URL space ID\&. When the number of compile-time
configured URL spaces runs out, a Tcl exception is raised\&.
.CS


 # Allocate a new URL space
 set newId [ns_urlspace new]

 # Use the new URL space ID
 ns_urlspace set -id $newId /*\&.php P

.CE
.PP
.SH "COMMON OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB-id\fR \fIinteger\fR
Specifies the ID of the URL space\&. Multiple different URL spaces can
be used, which can be allocated with the command
\fBns_urlspace new\fR\&. The maximum number of available URL spaces is a compile-time
constant of NaviServer\&. When no ID is specified, NaviServer
automatically allocates a default ID on first usage\&.
.TP
\fB-key\fR \fIvalue\fR
Every URL assignment can have a key, which is logically part of the
URL space\&. Using this key, you could, for example, assign different
values to a URL for different HTTP methods like \fIGET\fR or
\fIPOST\fR\&. The key can be used for other purposes as well\&. If no key is
specified, it defaults to the constant "\fB\&.\fR" (dot character)\&.
.PP
.SH EXAMPLES
.CS


 # Define mappings in the default URL space

 # Map all \&.adp files to "A"
 ns_urlspace set /*\&.adp A

 # Map all \&.html files to "H"
 ns_urlspace set /*\&.html H

 # Map a specific internal area to "I"
 ns_urlspace set /internal/space/* I

 # Query mappings
 ns_urlspace get /test\&.adp
 # Returns: A

 ns_urlspace get /foo/bar/test\&.adp
 # Returns: A

 ns_urlspace get /foo/baz/info\&.html
 # Returns: H

 ns_urlspace get /foo/baz/info\&.text
 # Returns: (no value assigned)

 ns_urlspace get /internal/space/documents/info\&.html
 # Returns: I

.CE
.SH "CONTEXT CONSTRAINTS"
In addition to the glob-based URL space matching, NaviServer supports
so-called \fIcontext constraints\fR\&. These constraints allow you to include additional
(context) information such as the current IP addresses or current
header fields in the URL space lookup\&. They’re applied after the
constraint-less URL match and
participate in the usual "most specific wins" resolution (i\&.e\&., if
there are multiple definitions for the same method and URL pattern
pair, having different context constraints, the most definition is taken)\&.
.PP
With context constraints, you can say "I want /foo\&.adp to map to
X only when this request comes from a Googlebot, and to Y
otherwise\&."  Or "serve internal pages only to clients in our
corporate subnet\&."
.PP
When you attach a context constraints in a connection-pool mapping,
or via \fBns_register_proc\fR, \fBns_register_filter\fR etc\&., the
live socket’s headers and peer address are used automatically when
deciding, which proc/filter/etc\&. is to be used\&. When you specify the
context constraints in a \fBns_urlspace set\fR command, you can pass
directly a context to a \fBns_urlspace set\fR command, by providing
an "ns_set" whose keys are header names (e\&.g\&. "user-agent") or the
special key "x-ns-ip" (pseudo header field) for IP/mask filters\&.
.PP
Context constraints may be \fIconjunctive\fR: if you supply
multiple key/value pairs, all must match\&.  Conjunctions are
specified as a flat list\&.
.PP
Example:
.CS


 # Base mapping when no context matches:
 % ns_urlspace set /*\&.adp DefaultHandler

 # When *either* user-agent contains "bot":
 % ns_urlspace set -constraints {user-agent *bot*} /*\&.adp BotHandler

 # When *both* UA="bot" *and* IP in 10\&.0\&.0\&.0/8:
 % ns_urlspace set -constraints {user-agent *bot* x-ns-ip 10\&.0\&.0\&.0/8} \\
                   /*\&.adp BotInternalHandler

 # Try without context:
 % ns_urlspace get /test\&.adp
 DefaultHandler

 # Try with only user-agent googlebot:
 % set ctx1 [ns_set create user-agent googlebot]
 % ns_urlspace get -context $ctx1 /test\&.adp
 BotHandler

 # Try with UA=googlebot *and* internal IP:
 % set ctx2 [ns_set create user-agent googlebot x-ns-ip 10\&.0\&.12\&.5]
 % ns_urlspace get -context $ctx2 /test\&.adp
 BotInternalHandler

.CE
See also the \fBns_server\fR configuration examples for defining
connection pools that use context constraints to route "*bot*" traffic
into a dedicated pool\&.
.PP
Context constraints can be as well set for the following commands
\fBns_register_adp\fR,
\fBns_register_cgi\fR,
\fBns_register_fastpath\fR,
\fBns_register_filter\fR, and
\fBns_register_proc\fR\&.
.SH NOTES
.PP
The URL patterns used in \fBns_urlspace\fR commands support
glob-style patterns, allowing for flexible matching\&. Be cautious when
defining patterns to ensure they match the intended URLs\&.
.PP
The URL space is stored in a trie data structure for efficient
matching\&.
.PP
When using context constraints, ensure that the context constraints of
the \fBns_urlspace set\fR command and the \fBns_set\fR (or request
headers) accurately represent the request conditions you want to
match\&. For security reasons, you should not rely solely on untrusted
request header fields\&.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ns_server, ns_set, nsv
.SH KEYWORDS
context constraints, data structure, server built-in, shared, trie, urlspace, variables
